        <div id="protein-per-calorie" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Protein per Calorie: The Number the Wrapper Hides
          </h2>

          <p>
            Protein per calorie is the fastest way to see whether a bar is a protein source or a snack. Divide the protein grams by the calories and multiply by 100. Quest's 21 g in 190 calories gives about 11 g per 100 calories; Barebells' 20 g in 200 calories gives 10 g; RXBAR's 12 g in about 210 calories gives under 6 g. The arithmetic takes ten seconds and changes how you see the shelf.
          </p>

          <p>
            For context, whey isolate mixed with water often lands near 22 to 25 g per 100 calories, and a typical whey concentrate around 20 g. Ready-to-drink shakes made from milk protein sit in the high teens. So even the best bars deliver roughly half the protein density of a powder shake. The extra calories come from fat, usually from nuts, nut butter or a chocolate coating, and from carbohydrate in the form of fiber and sugar alcohols. If you are cutting, a <a href="/best-protein-drink/">ready-to-drink protein shake</a> is usually the leaner portable option.
          </p>

          <p>
            That does not mean higher-calorie bars are bad. A bar with 20 g of protein and 220 calories makes a reasonable small meal when you are travelling, and the fat makes it more filling than a shake. The issue is only when bars become a way to hit a daily protein target. Three bars a day adds 550 to 650 calories to reach 60 g of protein, where three scoops of whey would add roughly 360.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch for serving-size tricks as well. Most of the bars here are a single 50 to 60 g serving, which is simple. Some snack-size bars and bites list protein per serving where a serving is less than the full package, and a few brands sell the same flavor in a smaller bar with a lower protein figure. Always check that the protein and calorie figures you are comparing are per whole bar.
          </p>

          <p>
            If protein per calorie is your priority, the dairy bars with erythritol or a low sugar-alcohol load are the efficient end of the market. Quest and Kirkland both clear 11 g per 100 calories with complete protein. Anything under 8 g per 100 calories should be judged as a snack, on taste and ingredients, rather than as a protein product.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sugar-alcohols" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Maltitol vs Erythritol vs Allulose
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sugar alcohols are why low-sugar bars can taste sweet. They are carbohydrates that the body absorbs only partly, so they add fewer calories than sugar and do not appear on the Sugars line. But they are not interchangeable, and the one a bar uses is the single biggest predictor of whether it sits comfortably in your stomach. US labels list total sugar alcohols on a separate line when a bar makes a sugar claim, and the specific one appears in the ingredient list.
          </p>

          <p>
            Maltitol is the most common in cheaper and candy-style bars because it behaves most like sugar in a recipe. It also has the strongest downsides. A sizeable share of maltitol is not absorbed in the small intestine, so it reaches the colon, where bacteria ferment it, producing gas, and it draws water into the gut, which can loosen stools. It also raises blood sugar more than other sugar alcohols, so a bar with 13 g of maltitol is not as blood-sugar neutral as its 1 g of sugar suggests. Barebells, Pure Protein and ONE all use it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Erythritol is the gentler option for most people. It is a small molecule that is largely absorbed in the small intestine and excreted unchanged in urine, so far less of it reaches the colon, and it has essentially no effect on blood sugar. Quest uses erythritol. One caveat: a 2023 observational study linked higher blood erythritol levels with cardiovascular events. It does not show cause and effect, and regulators still consider erythritol safe, but people with heart disease may want to discuss it with their doctor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Allulose is a rare sugar rather than a sugar alcohol, and it appears in a growing number of newer bars. It tastes very like sugar, contributes very few calories and, under FDA guidance, does not have to be counted in Total or Added Sugars on the label. Large amounts can still cause digestive symptoms in some people, but it is generally better tolerated than maltitol. If a bar lists allulose as its main sweetener, that is usually a point in its favor.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical rule: if a bar leaves you bloated, look at the sugar alcohol before blaming the protein. Switching from a maltitol bar to an erythritol or allulose bar, or to one with no sugar alcohols at all, fixes the problem for many people. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-sensitive-stomach/">guide for sensitive stomachs</a> covers the same issue in powders, where sucralose and stevia are more common than sugar alcohols.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Check the sugar alcohol, not just the sugar</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A bar with 1 g of sugar and 13 g of maltitol is not the same as a bar with 1 g of sugar and 6 g of erythritol. Maltitol causes more gas and bloating and raises blood sugar more. Read the ingredient list to see which one you are buying.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="fiber" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Fiber, Bloating and the Second-Bar Problem
          </h2>

          <p>
            Added fiber is the other half of the low-sugar formula. Bars use soluble corn fiber, polydextrose, isomalto-oligosaccharides, chicory root fiber or inulin to add bulk and chew without sugar. Fiber makes a bar more filling, which is a genuine benefit, and it lowers the net carbohydrate figure that keto-minded buyers look for. Quest's 12 g of fiber in a single bar is a big reason it holds you over longer than a Barebells.
          </p>

          <p>
            The trouble is dose and type. Twelve grams of added fiber at once is more than many people eat in a meal, and fermentable fibers such as inulin and chicory root are notorious for gas. Polydextrose and soluble corn fiber are usually better tolerated, but a second bar in the same afternoon doubles both the fiber and the sugar alcohol. That combination is why plenty of people find one bar fine and two bars uncomfortable.
          </p>

          <p>
            Isomalto-oligosaccharides, often shortened to IMO, deserve a specific note. They were widely used as a fiber in bars, but research has found that some IMO is digested like a starch, meaning it can raise blood sugar and contributes more calories than a true fiber would. FDA has not added IMO to its list of fibers that count as dietary fiber on labels, though some products still list it that way. If you are counting net carbs closely, a bar that relies heavily on IMO may affect you more than its fiber line suggests. ONE and some Kirkland formulations list it.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want fiber, a bar is a reasonable source, but it is not the only one. If you are trying to combine protein and fiber in one product, a powder built for that job can be gentler because you control the dose. Our sister guide to the <a href="/best-protein-powder-with-fiber/">best protein powder with fiber</a> covers that route, and a scoop of whey with a piece of fruit gets you there for less money.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a sensitive gut, the safest strategy is one bar at a time, with water, and preferably one that uses erythritol or allulose rather than maltitol, with a moderate fiber load. If even that causes trouble, RXBAR's date-and-nut approach avoids sugar alcohols and added fiber entirely, at the cost of protein density. Persistent digestive symptoms are worth raising with a doctor rather than solving bar by bar.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="protein-source" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Whey and Milk Protein vs Collagen Padding
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most bars build their protein from dairy: milk protein isolate, whey protein isolate, or a blend of the two. Milk protein isolate is roughly 80 percent casein and 20 percent whey, which digests slowly and gives bars their chewy texture. Whey isolate digests faster and carries the most leucine, the amino acid most closely tied to muscle protein synthesis. Either way, a dairy-first blend means the protein number on the wrapper is complete, high-quality protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some bars reach their headline figure partly with gelatin or collagen. Both are cheap, help with texture, and count toward the protein total on the label, because the label measures nitrogen rather than amino acid quality. But collagen and gelatin lack tryptophan entirely and are low in leucine, so they cannot support muscle in the way dairy protein does. A bar with 20 g of protein, a quarter of it from collagen, is effectively delivering less useful protein than the label suggests.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pure Protein's ingredient lists we checked include gelatin, and BUILT Puff describes its protein as a whey and collagen blend. Neither is hiding anything: both appear on the ingredient list. The point is to read it. If gelatin, collagen, hydrolyzed collagen or collagen peptides appear in the protein blend, especially near the top, discount the protein figure in your head. Our <a href="/best-collagen-protein-powder/">collagen guide</a> explains what collagen is and is not good for.
          </p>

          <p>
            Other protein sources appear too. Soy protein isolate is complete and common in budget bars. Egg white protein, as in RXBAR, is complete and highly digestible. Plant bars typically use pea, rice, or a blend; check that a blend is used, since single plant proteins can be low in one amino acid. Nut butters add a few grams but mostly add fat, so a bar whose first ingredients are nuts and dates will always be lower in protein than one led by an isolate.
          </p>

          <p>
            The ranking reflects this. Quest and Kirkland lead because their protein is milk and whey isolate first, at a reasonable calorie cost. Barebells and ONE use dairy isolates too and lose ground mainly on sweetener. Pure Protein and BUILT Puff rank lower because of the collagen or gelatin share, even though their wrappers look competitive.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Cost per 25 g: Bars vs Powder
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cost per 25 g of protein is the fairest way to compare a box of bars with a tub of powder. Take the box price, divide by the number of bars, divide by the protein per bar and multiply by 25. At $35.49 for 12, Quest works out to about $2.96 a bar and about $3.50 per 25 g. At roughly $22 to $24 for 20, Kirkland works out to about $1.10 to $1.20 a bar and $1.30 to $1.45 per 25 g.
          </p>

          <p>
            For comparison, mainstream whey in a large tub typically costs around $1.40 to $1.50 per 25 g at regular prices and less on sale. That puts Kirkland bars level with whey powder, which is unusual, and Pure Protein not far behind at about $1.55 to $1.80. Barebells, at about $2.90, and Quest at full brand-site price, at about $3.50, cost two to two and a half times as much as powder for the same protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            Prices move a lot in this category. Quest and RXBAR are frequently discounted on Amazon and at grocery chains, and RXBAR's 12-count box has dropped from a list price near $21 to around $13 with Subscribe and Save. Barebells ranges from under $28 for 12 at Target to much more from third-party sellers. Buying in boxes of 12 or more, rather than single bars at a checkout, usually halves the cost per bar. Our <a href="/best-costco-protein-powder/">Costco protein guide</a> covers the club-store side.
          </p>

          <p>
            Remember that cost per 25 g only measures protein. A bar also delivers 140 to 220 calories of food, which has some value if it replaces a snack you would have bought anyway. If a bar stops you buying a $4 pastry, the comparison is not with whey powder but with the pastry. If you are buying bars to hit a protein target, though, powder or a bottled shake is almost always cheaper per useful gram.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Approx. cost per 25 g of protein</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$1.30-1.45</p>
