            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="when-to-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. When a Bar Makes Sense, and When to Buy Powder
          </h2>

          <p>
            Bars earn their place in specific situations: travel, long days away from a kitchen, a bag you carry to the gym, a desk drawer, a hike, or a commute where a shaker bottle would leak. In those cases the convenience is worth paying for, and a bar with 20 g of complete protein beats skipping a meal or grabbing a pastry. One bar a day is a reasonable habit for most people.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bars make less sense as the backbone of your protein intake. Most adults who exercise aim for somewhere around 1.2 to 1.6 g of protein per kilogram of body weight a day, and our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">guide to how much protein you need</a> explains how to set your own target. Filling a 30 to 50 g gap with bars costs more calories, more sugar alcohol and usually more money than doing it with food or a shake. A tub of whey or a ready-to-drink shake is the better daily tool.
          </p>

          <p>
            For weight loss, the calorie density of bars matters. A 200-calorie bar is a reasonable snack, but it is easy to treat it as free because it is labelled protein. If you are in a calorie deficit, the leaner bars here, or a shake, give you more protein per calorie. For muscle gain, bars are a convenient extra, and their calories are less of a concern, but the protein source still matters: dairy-first bars are the ones that actually support training.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some people should be more careful. Anyone with irritable bowel syndrome may react strongly to maltitol and fermentable fibers. People managing diabetes should note that maltitol and IMO can raise blood sugar more than the net-carb figure implies, and should check with their care team before relying on bars. Anyone with kidney disease, a milk, soy, peanut or tree-nut allergy, or who is pregnant, should check labels carefully and ask a doctor or registered dietitian. This guide is not medical advice.
          </p>

          <p>
            The simplest way to use this guide: keep a box of one good bar for the situations where nothing else works, and buy your everyday protein as powder or shakes. That combination costs less, sits better in most stomachs, and leaves bars doing the job they are genuinely good at.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Judging a bar by the front-label protein number</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most mainstream bars land between 15 and 21 g, so the headline barely separates them. What matters is the protein per calorie, the sugar alcohol, the fiber load and whether part of the protein is collagen or gelatin. Turn the box over and read the ingredient list before you compare two bars on protein alone.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming low sugar means stomach-friendly</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A bar can list 1 g of sugar and still contain 13 g of maltitol, the sugar alcohol most likely to cause gas, bloating and loose stools. Look at the Sugar Alcohol line and the ingredient list. Erythritol and allulose are usually gentler, and a bar with no sugar alcohols avoids the problem entirely.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Counting collagen and gelatin protein like whey</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Collagen and gelatin count toward the label's protein total, but they lack tryptophan and are low in leucine, so they do little for muscle. If collagen, gelatin or collagen peptides appear in a bar's protein blend, mentally discount the protein figure, and favor bars that list milk or whey protein isolate first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Eating two or three bars a day to hit a target</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Bars carry 140 to 220 calories each, plus sugar alcohols and added fiber. Three a day adds 550 to 650 calories and a dose of maltitol or fiber that many stomachs object to. Use one bar where convenience matters, and get the rest of your protein from food, powder or a ready-to-drink shake.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying single bars at the checkout</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Single bars at convenience-store prices can cost double what the same bar costs in a 12- or 20-count box. Convert to cost per 25 g of protein and the difference is obvious. Buying a box on sale, through a subscription discount or at a warehouse club brings some bars close to whey powder on price.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is the healthiest protein bar?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  There is no single healthiest bar, but the best labels combine about 20 g of milk or whey protein, under 200 calories, low sugar and a gentle sweetener such as erythritol or allulose. Quest fits that well. If you want to avoid sweeteners and sugar alcohols entirely, RXBAR's short ingredient list is cleaner, though it delivers far less protein per calorie than dairy bars.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Are protein bars good for weight loss?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  They can help if a bar replaces a higher-calorie snack, because protein and fiber are filling. But most bars carry 180 to 220 calories, so they are not free. For the most protein per calorie, a ready-to-drink shake or whey mixed with water is leaner. If you use bars while dieting, count them like food and stick to one a day.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Why do protein bars make me bloated?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  The usual causes are sugar alcohols, especially maltitol, and large doses of added fiber such as inulin, chicory root or polydextrose. Both ferment in the colon and produce gas. Try a bar sweetened with erythritol or allulose, eat one at a time with water, or choose a bar without sugar alcohols. If symptoms persist, speak to a doctor rather than assuming it is the protein.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is it OK to eat a protein bar every day?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For most healthy adults, one bar a day is fine as a snack or travel option. The concerns are cumulative: calories, sugar alcohols and added fiber add up if you eat several. A bar should not replace whole meals regularly. People with kidney disease, diabetes, food allergies or who are pregnant should check with a doctor or registered dietitian first.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Are protein bars cheaper than protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Usually not. Mainstream whey costs about $1.40 to $1.50 per 25 g of protein, while branded bars often run $2.50 to $3.50 per 25 g at full price. The exception is bulk buying: Kirkland bars at Costco work out around $1.30 to $1.45 per 25 g, and Pure Protein boxes about $1.55 to $1.80, close to powder.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is the difference between maltitol and erythritol?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Both are sugar alcohols, but they behave differently. Much of the maltitol you eat reaches the colon, where it ferments and can cause gas and loose stools, and it raises blood sugar noticeably. Erythritol is mostly absorbed and excreted in urine, so it causes fewer digestive symptoms and has almost no blood-sugar effect. Most people tolerate erythritol bars better than maltitol bars.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Do collagen protein bars build muscle?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Not as well as dairy bars. Collagen is incomplete, with no tryptophan and little leucine, so it contributes far less to muscle protein synthesis than whey or milk protein. A bar that mixes collagen with whey still offers some useful protein, but less than its label total implies. For muscle, choose bars with milk or whey protein isolate listed first.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
        </div>

